<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernward of Hildesheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 960–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Bernward</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernward was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> from 993 to 1022, after serving as tutor to the young Emperor Otto III. A patron of art and building, he founded the church of St Michael and commissioned the bronze doors of 1015 and a bronze column, masterpieces of Ottonian art. He was canonised in 1192.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernward came from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble family; his grandfather was Athelbero, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Orphaned young, he was placed in the care of his uncle Volkmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Utrecht, who entrusted his education to the scholar Thangmar. Under him Bernward studied the sciences and the liberal and mechanical arts, and became skilled in mathematics, painting, architecture and above all in making church vessels and ornaments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold.</p><p>He completed his studies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, where Archbishop Willigis, Chancellor of the Empire, ordained him priest. He turned down a valuable post in his uncle's diocese to stay with his ageing grandfather. After Athelbero's death in 987 he became a chaplain at the imperial court, and soon afterwards the Empress-Regent Theophano made him tutor to her son Otto III, then six years old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bernward was the thirteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>. His years in office fell in the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> emperors, whose family came from the region around Hildesheim and were related to him, and the city was then a centre of power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. He set out to give it an appearance to match, built many churches and other buildings, and revived a classical practice by having his name stamped on the roof tiles made under his direction.</p><p>He also fortified his see. The cathedral district received a strong wall with twelve towers, and further forts in the countryside guarded against raids by neighbouring Slavic peoples and against the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, from whose attacks he defended his diocese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bernward died on 20 November 1022, a few weeks after the consecration of St Michael's Church, which he had built on a symmetrical plan with two apses typical of Ottonian Romanesque art in Old Saxony. He is buried in its western crypt, and his feast day is 20 November. Thangmar wrote his life, the Vita Bernwardi, though parts of it were probably added in the High Middle Ages.</p><p>The bronze doors he commissioned, now at St Mary's Cathedral, show the Fall of Man and the life of Christ and are related in some ways to the wooden doors of Santa Sabina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. St Michael's Church and St Mary's Cathedral, with their treasures, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, and churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernward of Hildesheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bishoprics such as Hildesheim flourished, and Bishop <b>Bernward</b> commissioned its celebrated bronze doors in 1015.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1000 Bishop <b>Bernward</b> made the city a centre of art, building St. Michael's Church and commissioning its bronze doors and column.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-hildesheim">Prince-Bishopric of Hildesheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Saxony. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.15,9.95&amp;year=1000" aria-label="Hildesheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 362 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bernward of Hildesheim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 632h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.15,9.95&amp;year=1000">What was here in 1000? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
